<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  A TEMPLATE, not a loadable job. Every {ROOT} below is a placeholder.

  Render it with the project folder written in, and it becomes a plist launchd
  will accept. From the project folder:

    sed "s|{ROOT}|$PWD|g" config/launchd/templates/backup_db.plist.tmpl \
      > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.mtalib.agentic-trading.backup_db.plist
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) \
      ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.mtalib.agentic-trading.backup_db.plist

  WHY THIS ONE IS NOT GENERATED LIKE THE SIX LIVE JOBS. Those are written by
  scripts/gen_launchd.py from config/launchd/templates/*.template, and
  tests/test_paths.py checks that every generated plist matches its template and
  that each job wakes up exactly the number of times it is meant to. This job
  has never run on a schedule, so it is deliberately kept out of that set,
  exactly like deadman.plist.tmpl beside it.

  When Mo decides to arm it, the move is small and worth doing properly:

    1. write config/launchd/templates/backup_db.template in gen_launchd's own
       format, with the program, environment and comment from this file, and the
       schedule line reading exactly:
         at 17:00 on everyday
    2. add "backup_db": 7 to EXPECTED_WAKE_UPS in tests/test_paths.py
    3. run scripts/gen_launchd.py to write the plist, then run it again with the
       install flag to load it

  WHAT IT DOES. At 17:00 every day, including weekends, it takes one copy of
  data/trading.sqlite into data/backups/trading_YYYY-MM-DD.sqlite and deletes
  any copy more than thirty days old. One line goes into output/backup_db.log
  saying what it did.

  WHY EVERY DAY AND NOT ONLY WEEKDAYS. A weekend copy holds no new trades, and
  that is fine: the point of running it daily is that the thirty day window is
  thirty real days rather than six working weeks, and that a Mac that dies on a
  Sunday still has Friday's data in a file written by a job that was clearly
  working the day before.

  WHY 17:00. The market shuts at 16:00 and the Google Sheet sync runs at 16:35,
  so by five o'clock the day is finished and written down. The backup is taken
  after all of it.

  IT CANNOT TRADE, and it cannot damage the live database. It only ever reads
  the database and writes a new file beside it. It uses SQLite's own online
  backup rather than a file copy, which is the point: the database runs in WAL
  mode, so a plain copy taken mid write would quietly miss the newest rows or
  produce a torn file that still looks fine.

  launchd works in this Mac's own local time, and there is no setting in here
  that pins a time zone, so the time below is this Mac's clock. It does not
  matter much for a backup, but keep the Mac on Eastern like everything else.

  NOT LOADED by writing this file. Loading is the separate, deliberate step
  written out above.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.mtalib.agentic-trading.backup_db</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
      <string>{ROOT}/scripts/backup_db.sh</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>{ROOT}</string>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>{ROOT}/output/launchd_backup_db.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>{ROOT}/output/launchd_backup_db.err.log</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
      <key>AGENTIC_TRADING_ROOT</key><string>{ROOT}</string>
      <key>PATH</key><string>/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin</string>
      <key>TZ</key><string>America/New_York</string>
    </dict>

    <!-- A copy of a database this size takes under a second, so two minutes is
         already far more than it can ever need. -->
    <key>AbandonProcessGroup</key>
    <false/>
    <key>ExitTimeOut</key>
    <integer>120</integer>

    <!-- Do not fire the moment the job is loaded, only at 17:00. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <!-- 7 wake ups a week: 17:00, every day. -->
    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <array>
      <dict>
        <key>Weekday</key><integer>0</integer>
        <key>Hour</key><integer>17</integer>
        <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
      </dict>
      <dict>
        <key>Weekday</key><integer>1</integer>
        <key>Hour</key><integer>17</integer>
        <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
      </dict>
      <dict>
        <key>Weekday</key><integer>2</integer>
        <key>Hour</key><integer>17</integer>
        <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
      </dict>
      <dict>
        <key>Weekday</key><integer>3</integer>
        <key>Hour</key><integer>17</integer>
        <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
      </dict>
      <dict>
        <key>Weekday</key><integer>4</integer>
        <key>Hour</key><integer>17</integer>
        <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
      </dict>
      <dict>
        <key>Weekday</key><integer>5</integer>
        <key>Hour</key><integer>17</integer>
        <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
      </dict>
      <dict>
        <key>Weekday</key><integer>6</integer>
        <key>Hour</key><integer>17</integer>
        <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>
      </dict>
    </array>

</dict>
</plist>
